  "body": "# AGIJobManager v1.9.1 — Current guides and recovery-only tooling\n\nThis release addresses stale operating instructions behind the previous edition's version banners.\n\n- **Updated main README.** A concise system overview explains general keyboard/mouse/screen jobs, implemented behavior, private companion editions, no-retainer admission, payouts, setup and actual commissioning boundaries.\n- **Generated current guides.** README, companion guide, release scope and complete root/Hardhat/UI command inventory derive from checked package metadata. Current downloads and visible labels agree.\n- **Correct admission reference.** New work uses calibrated schema 6/7 `operator-budget` policies. CLI help and the synthetic example include all five fields, including the exact UTF-8 evidence-report string. Historical analysis remains separate from live admission.\n- **Recovery-only review escrow.** Fresh deployment is disabled. Renamed `recover:review-escrow:*` commands check the original journal, receipt, runtime and independent RPC before source verification, without obtaining a signer. Existing credits and refund rights are preserved.\n- **Documentation regression gates.** Checks reject stale visible labels, obsolete commands, unsupported current schemas and missing local files/heading targets. New tests reproduce the drift classes found by the audit.\n- **Current computer-use coverage.** Integration notes distinguish available provider and OpenClaw native/node capabilities from the isolated public-work adapter included with the private packages.\n\nPrivate companions are Fleet 1.7.1, Agent 1.13.1 and Node 2.14.1. Their current READMEs, setup folders, partner versions, public schema copies, command inventories and offline HTML section navigation are repaired. Source-digest checks detect stale rendered guides. Historical evidence and contract provenance retain their original identities.\n\nThe qualified source passed 688 contract/tool tests, 113 deployment preflight tests, all five required workflows, UI/security checks and 34 historical native-USDC/ENS fork cases. The private packages passed 415 unit tests and 102 offline guide-page checks. See `VALIDATION.md` for exact scope.\n\nAll Solidity, payout rules and dependency resolutions are unchanged. This release does not deploy a contract, open intake, establish actual M1/M2 operation, or demonstrate universal task performance. Native signed-in/private-account adapters remain unimplemented in the bundled private runtime. Live provider quality, independent validation, actual costs and sustained useful settlements still require commissioning.\n"
